Fillets: a walk that stops on a restriction of one surface where its two
contacts meet gives the other contact's extremity the walk's parameter,
not the V of that contact on its surface (mitcad#1346).

Where BRepBlend_Walking stops on a restriction of one surface (Blend_OnRst1
or Blend_OnRst2) with its two contacts at one point, the blend vanishing
there, the extremity on the other surface is set by the four-argument
BRepBlend_Extremity::SetValue, the overload for a point on a restriction
(P, W, Param, Tol): the contact's U went into W and its V into the
parameter on the guide. ChFi3d_FilCommonPoint takes that for the common
point's parameter, and where the contact's end lies on an arc of its face
(the end at a vertex) StoreData's CompParam looks for the arc on the
contact's curve on the face at it; with the curve evaluated far outside its
range there, neither the intersection nor the projection found it, and the
end took the wrong parameter. The contact's edge then ended part of the way
along, at the blend's other side, and the end's degenerated edge was made
from there across the blend: the faces' wires crossed.

A plate's side rounded with 0.3 along an earlier rounding of its top edge
that vanishes into the side (the two faces tangent at the vertex where it
ends): the walk reaches the vertex with no width left there, stopping on
the side's edge, and the contact on the earlier rounding got that
rounding's V there (-0.034, the vertex's parameter on its edge) instead of
36.96. Both such extremities now take previousP.Parameter(), as every
other extremity of the walk does.

Test: geometry/tests/test_fillet_vanishing_side.cpp, the reported plate's
sides rounded on the body MitCad builds: the result is valid and its faces
lie on the stored body after the rounding within 0.01 mm (invalid without
the patch, the faces' wires crossing at both vanishing ends).

Upstream: new.

